WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:40.000
Meme Forge helps teams test a complete commerce journey without connecting real payments or shipping.
This recording uses a local production build and disposable PostgreSQL, not a live Azure deployment.

00:40.000 --> 01:00.000
The version-controlled catalog supplies product context and prices.
Choose Ship It and Senior Duck Engineer for the reference journey.

01:00.000 --> 01:35.000
Add Ship It. The cart opens and shows the selected product.
The browser holds cart state; it is not an inventory or payment system.

01:35.000 --> 02:05.000
Add Senior Duck Engineer. One of each totals $9.98.
Checkout submits product identifiers and quantities, not trusted prices.

02:05.000 --> 02:40.000
Adjust the quantity and restore one of each.
The server independently resolves prices before persisting the order.

02:40.000 --> 03:20.000
Create the test order. No payment is collected and fulfillment is mocked.
This generic confirmation page is not independent proof of database persistence.

03:20.000 --> 04:00.000
Presenter evidence now shows a real database read for this checkout and the live health response.
Only product names, quantities, amounts, and status are shown; identifiers and tokens are omitted.

04:00.000 --> 04:30.000
The architecture separates the application from optional governed delivery.
Azure deployment and external governance require separate approval and verification.

04:30.000 --> 05:00.000
Next step: bring a product owner and platform lead to choose one customer journey and a bounded pilot.
Agree on integration dependencies, security controls, ownership, and measurable acceptance criteria.
